Aster OS window manager (WM) configuration

This directory holds everything that customizes the desktop shell at runtime.
The whole shell is written in Lua and lives inside the kernel; these files are
the user-facing configuration surface (loaded from the disk, applied live).

Layout:
  README       this file — how the WM is configured and extended
  theme.lua    colors and geometry (hot-reloadable, see below)
  api.lua      the Lua API reference the shell exposes
  .theme.bak   manual backup of the last Ctrl+S (read-only, never loaded)

Hot reload
  Edit /wm/theme.lua and save it (Ctrl+S in the editor, Super+Z opens it) —
  the desktop repaints immediately, no F5 and no kernel rebuild. If the new
  theme does not parse or run, the change is reported in the REPL and the
  shell keeps the built-in defaults; the system never crashes. To restore a
  previous save, view .theme.bak (Space in the file browser) and copy its
  content back into theme.lua manually — backups are never loaded
  automatically.

Opening files
  Super+E opens the file browser at the root. Enter opens a file, Space views
  it read-only, Delete removes it. Super+T opens the editor with a fresh
  buffer; Super+Z opens /wm/theme.lua directly.

Changing window behaviour
  theme.lua controls colors and geometry (what the shell draws). To change
  which windows exist, the keybindings, the launcher entries or the taskbar,
  the WM exposes its Lua API — see api.lua and the shell source modules
  (wm.lua, input.lua, launcher.lua) for the hooks you can call from Lua.
